WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
SPEAKER 1: Hi, my
name is Shane Glass,

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.830
and I'm the program manager for
Google Cloud's Public Datasets

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.860
Program.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.160
I'm here today to talk to
you about the public datasets

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.220
we host in BigQuery and
Google Cloud Storage,

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.290
and how these datasets
can help you more easily

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.950
unlock new insights
in your existing data.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.140
We currently have more than 130
datasets in the Public Datasets

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.240
Program.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.290
These datasets are onboarded
and maintained by Googlers

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:26.665
with input and guidance
from data providers.

00:00:26.665 --> 00:00:29.040
You can see here a sample of
our weather and climate data

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.880
providers, including NOAA,
NASA, the European Space

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.610
Agency, and the US Geological
Survey, among others.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.130
Our BigQuery public
datasets can be

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.680
joined with your existing
data to help you uncover

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.570
new insights more easily.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.180
Datasets like the Global
Surface Summary of the Day,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.520
which provides meteorological
observations of weather

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.680
stations around
the world every day

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.260
going back more than 100
years, can for instance,

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.770
help you better understand
how weather might impact sales

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.458
patterns for your business.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.000
We're constantly
adding new datasets,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.770
and here are just two
examples of recently

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.959
added unstructured
datasets, both coming

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:09.100
from NOAA, the National Oceanic
and Atmospheric Administration.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.380
These are the
High-Resolution Rapid Refresh

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.360
Model, which includes
fine-grained wind

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.780
data at various altitudes,
and the National Water Model,

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.940
which has information
about flooding and water

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.580
moving across the
continental United States.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.190
You can learn more about the
data we make available for use

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.110
in the GCP Marketplace.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.600
Here, you'll find an overview
of the data and samples

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.200
to help you get started.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.530
You can go there now to
explore what's available

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.660
and start learning
more from your data.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:37.450
Thank you.

